# Dart Best Practices

Follow Effective Dart. Sound null safety: prefer explicit `T`/`T?`; avoid `!` unless the value is guaranteed non-null.

- `final` when there is no reassignment; `const` whenever possible.
- Immutable data and widgets when practical.
- Exhaustive `switch`; use patterns and records when they clarify code.
- Arrow functions only for simple one-liners. One responsibility per function.
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Meaningful names; avoid cryptic abbreviations.

## Naming

- Classes: `PascalCase`
- Members: `camelCase`
- Files/folders: `snake_case`

Keep functions short. Flatten deep nesting. Comments explain **why**, not obvious **what**. Document public APIs when it helps callers.

```dart
// ❌ BAD
try { await fetchData(); } catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await fetchData();
} catch (e, st) {
  AppLogger.error('Failed to fetch: $e');
  emit(HomeError(e.toString()));
}
```
